  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What to check first</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Contact details</strong>CRM records are full of personal information. This needs its own review.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer confidentiality</strong>Pricing and contract terms with customers are often restricted.</li>
        <li><strong>Data quality</strong>Records with consistent stages and notes are easier to assess.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a deal history teaches a model</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A CRM export is mostly outcomes: stage, amount, close date. The part worth licensing is the notes, where a salesperson recorded what the buyer objected to and why the deal was finally lost.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>How qualification happened</strong>The questions asked before a deal was worked, and the answers that ruled it in or out.</li>
        <li><strong>The stated reason for a loss</strong>The recorded cause, which is usually more specific and less flattering than the pipeline report.</li>
        <li><strong>What the buyer asked for before signing</strong>Security review, references, pilot terms and the order they arrived in.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What keeps CRM records out of scope</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A contact list is the least useful part of a CRM here, and the most legally awkward. The value sits in the deal notes around it.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Contact lists on their own</strong>Names, emails and phone numbers are personal data, and marketing rules travel with them.</li>
        <li><strong>Named-customer contract terms</strong>Pricing and negotiated terms with an identified customer are usually confidential to that customer.</li>
        <li><strong>Forecasts and opinions</strong>A projection is not a record of work done, and it adds nothing a buyer can learn from.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

